1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
This project starts with a real child

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,500
living with Mosaic Variegated Aneuploidy —

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,000
an ultra-rare disease with no approved treatment.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,500
In Track 1, we found a likely causal genetic diagnosis.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:20,000
This pitch is about two already-approved drugs worth investigating.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,500
The proband carries two damaged copies of BUB1B —

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,500
one is a known pathogenic stop-gained variant, already in ClinVar,

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,000
the other a novel missense variant predicted deleterious by SIFT and PolyPhen.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,500
BUB1B encodes BUBR1, a core protein of the mitotic spindle checkpoint —

10
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:42,000
the quality control system that stops a cell from dividing

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,500
until every chromosome is properly attached.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,500
When BUBR1 drops below a functional threshold,

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,000
cells can no longer reliably hold the checkpoint.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Chromosomes separate too early, cells end up with the wrong chromosome number,

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
and that instability — repeated across a lifetime of cell divisions —

16
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,500
is what predisposes these children to cancers like rhabdomyosarcoma.

17
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,000
This isn't just one paper's claim:

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,500
we pulled the interaction network from STRING and the pathway data from Reactome,

19
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:25,500
and BUB1B sits at the center of exactly this checkpoint module, independently, in both databases.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
So the question for Track 2 isn't "how do we fix the checkpoint" —

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,500
it's "given a cell that already has a weak checkpoint,

22
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,500
what already-approved drug exploits that weakness?"

23
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,000
We found two candidates, each backed by a specific mechanistic study, not a hunch.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Bosutinib, an approved leukemia drug, was shown in an independent screen

25
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,500
to be selectively toxic to cells with a defective spindle checkpoint —

26
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:59,000
exactly this cell's biology.

27
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,500
Metformin, one of the most widely used and safest drugs in the world,

28
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:08,000
was shown in a separate screen to selectively stress aneuploid cells

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
through the AMPK energy-sensing pathway.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,500
Both are cheap, generic, and already deeply characterized in humans —

31
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,500
which matters enormously for an ultra-rare disease with no dedicated drug pipeline.

32
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,999
And just as important:

33
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,500
we explicitly ruled out a whole class of drugs — checkpoint inhibitors

34
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,000
already in cancer development —

35
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,500
because in this patient they'd push in exactly the wrong direction.

36
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:33,000
This mechanism isn't unique to this one mutation.

37
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
It generalizes to the other two known MVA genes,

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,500
and to chromosomally unstable tumors more broadly —

39
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:46,500
the same vulnerability has independently shown up in glioblastoma and prostate cancer research.

40
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:51,000
The pipeline we used — phenotype, to mechanism, to literature-mined approved drugs —

41
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
is reusable for the next undiagnosed case, not just this one.

42
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,500
These are hypotheses for follow-up, not proven treatments —

43
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:02,000
validating them in this child's own cells is the obvious next step.

44
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
But for a family that had no genetic answer six weeks ago,

45
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
we think this is a real, testable place to start.
